{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 106, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding the input-to-output residual where none existed gives large gains, while replacing an existing op on edge 3 gives small or negative changes."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing parameter-free pooling on the last edge with a 3x3 conv adds learnable capacity and consistently helps, sometimes hugely."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "direction": "lower", "why": "Swapping a 1x1 conv for a 3x3 conv adds little capacity and was slightly negative on all datasets, unlike replacing pool, skip, or none."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing pooling on the first edge with a conv yields very large gains in weak cells, while replacing a conv is roughly neutral."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 5 absent, removing edge 4 leaves the output with few or no paths, so the accuracy drop is far larger."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 4 absent, removing edge 5 starves the output node of inputs and is catastrophic."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Without the 0->3 skip, an identity on edge 5 supplies a needed residual path, whereas with that skip present it removes conv capacity."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When the input-to-output skip is missing, a skip on edge 4 adds a valuable shortcut; otherwise it is neutral or harmful."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "